n type that looks clever up close. If you are supplying artwork yourself, the <a href=\"/best-vehicle-wrap-file-types/\">file types guide</a> covers resolution and formats.</p>\n<p>Once the proof is approved, printed graphics are produced and laminated. Colour-change film is cut from stock rolls, so that stage of a truck wrap installation is shorter.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: How to prepare your truck for drop-off</h2>\n<p>What you do before a truck wrap installation has a direct effect on how well the film bonds. 3M''s <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/2419563O/application-vehicles-ra-pdf.pdf\">Application: Vehicles instruction bulletin</a> treats every vehicle surface as contaminated until it has been cleaned immediately before the film goes on, and it singles out car wax as something that can greatly reduce adhesion. A few things to do and avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wash the truck</strong> a day or two before, including the wheel arches, the tailgate and the lower doors where road film collects. Our installers will clean every panel again, but they should be removing fingerprints, not a season of mud.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not wax, polish or apply a ceramic coating</strong> beforehand. Any wax has to be stripped off with solvent before the film can stick, and a fresh coating makes that harder.</li>\n<li><strong>Skip the spray-wax and spot-free rinse options</strong> at an automatic wash. 3M''s bulletin warns that the chemicals used in some automated washing equipment can prevent good film adhesion.</li>\n<li><strong>Empty the cab and the bed</strong> of anything you need. The truck will be in the shop for the duration of the job.</li>\n<li><strong>Tell the shop about repairs, repaints and rust.</strong> Film will not bond reliably to flaking paint or active rust, and fresh paint needs time to cure before it is wrapped. Our article on <a href=\"/rust-repair-before-vehicle-wrap-toronto/\">repairing rust before a wrap</a> explains what has to happen first.</li>\n<li><strong>Mention aftermarket parts.</strong> Toolboxes, ladder racks, bed covers, mud flaps, fender flares and old decals all affect how the film is laid out, and some have to come off for the install.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At drop-off, expect a walkaround. 3M recommends that installers complete a written pre-installation inspection record for every vehicle, noting existing chips, scratches and unsound paint. This protects both sides: you know what was already there, and the installer knows which areas need extra care or cannot be covered by a warranty.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: Inside the shop during a truck wrap installation</h2>\n<p>A truck wrap installation happens in a clean, well-lit, temperature-controlled bay, and the reasons are practical rather than cosmetic.</p>\n<h3>Temperature matters more than you think</h3>\n<p>3M''s best-practice guidance is to apply vehicle graphics when both the air and the vehicle''s surface are between 16°C and 32°C. Below that range the film cools too quickly to stretch into recesses and the initial bond may not hold; above it the adhesive becomes more aggressive and the film can pre-adhere and trap air. Avery Dennison''s <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/eu/en/Data-Sheets/Supreme-Wrap/PDS-Supreme-Wrapping-Film-EN.pdf\">Supreme Wrapping Film data sheet</a> sets a hard minimum application temperature of 10°C. That is why a January install in Toronto has to happen indoors, and why a truck that has sat outside overnight is brought in to warm up before anyone touches it.</p>\n<h3>Cleaning, again</h3>\n<p>Even a truck that arrived spotless is cleaned panel by panel immediately before the film is applied: a detergent wash, a thorough dry, and a solvent wipe to remove any trace of wax, oil or polish. Dust that settles between the cleaning and the application is enough to cause lifting later, so the two steps happen back to back.</p>\n<h3>Laying the film</h3>\n<p>Installers work panel by panel, heating the film to conform it around mirrors, bumper corners and the deep channels on a pickup bed, then squeegeeing it down with firm, overlapping strokes. The film is cut at every body seam so that panels can flex independently, and the edges are post-heated and squeegeed again to lock them down. Where a cut has to be made over paint, our installers use knifeless tape rather than a blade, which our article on <a href=\"/knifeless-tape-car-wrap/\">knifeless tape wraps</a> explains in detail. The process we follow on every vehicle is described in <a href=\"/car-wrap-how-we-do-it-at-vwt/\">how we do it at Vinyl Wrap Toronto</a>.</p>\n<h2>Step 5: How long the truck will be in the shop</h2>\n<p>There is no single answer to how long a truck wrap installation takes, which is why a quote comes with its own timeline. As our FAQ notes, turnaround can run from an hour for simple lettering to as long as two weeks for a complex job. The factors that move it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Coverage.</strong> Lettering is the quickest job, a full wrap on a crew-cab pickup is the longest common one, and a box truck is more surface again.</li>\n<li><strong>Complexity.</strong> Deep recesses, rivets, corrugated box panels and lots of trim all slow the job down.</li>\n<li><strong>Removal.</strong> Old decals or a previous wrap must be stripped and the adhesive cleaned off before the new film goes on. See <a href=\"/how-to-remove-truck-decals/\">how to remove truck decals</a> for what that involves.</li>\n<li><strong>Repairs.</strong> Dents, rust and failing paint have to be fixed first, and that is usually a separate trade.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plan for the truck to be off the road for the full quoted window, and ask the shop to flag you early if removal or repairs push that out.</p>\n<h2>Step 6: Pickup and the first few days</h2>\n<p>Pickup starts with another walkaround, this time with the finished wrap. Look at the edges, the seams and the areas around handles and lights, and ask about anything that catches your eye while the truck is still in the bay. This is also the time to confirm what warranty applies to your film and your installation. The <a href=\"/truck-wraps/\">truck wraps</a> page states what is included on our jobs, and our article on <a href=\"/certified-vinyl-wrap-installer-3m-vs-avery/\">certified installers</a> explains how manufacturer-backed warranties work.</p>\n<p>The adhesive keeps building its bond after the truck leaves, and the manufacturers are specific about what to do in the days after a truck wrap installation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keep it warm.</strong> 3M''s vehicle application bulletin says to keep the vehicle above 16°C for at least 12 hours before exposing it to cold or wet conditions, because that strengthens the bond in contoured areas. Avery Dennison''s <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/na/en/documents/Instructional-Bulletins/General-Information/ib150-cleaning-maintenance-vehicle-wraps.pdf\">cleaning and maintenance bulletin</a> goes further for winter work and asks for 24 hours at room temperature before the vehicle goes back out into the cold. In a Toronto winter, that may mean leaving the truck at the shop overnight.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not wash it yet.</strong> 3M''s minimum is 24 hours after a graphics installation, and its care guidance for 2080 colour-change film extends that to 72 hours. Your installer will tell you which applies to your film.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand wash, or touchless.</strong> When you do wash, hand washing is best. If you use an automatic wash, choose a touchless one; brushes can dull the film and lift edges.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep pressure washers away from edges.</strong> Both manufacturers allow pressure washing within limits, but both warn that water forced at an edge will lift it. Our <a href=\"/van-wrap-cleaning-guide/\">wrap cleaning guide</a> covers safe washing methods in detail.</li>\n<li><strong>Wipe fuel spills immediately.</strong> Diesel and gasoline degrade the vinyl and the adhesive if they are left to sit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>From there, the wrap wants the same care as paint: regular washing, no wax or polish on matte or textured films, and an ice scraper kept well away from it in winter. Our guides to <a href=\"/remove-ice-and-snow-from-a-wrapped-vehicle/\">clearing ice and snow from a wrapped vehicle</a> and <a href=\"/top-8-maintenance-tips-for-vehicle-wraps/\">long-term wrap maintenance</a> cover the rest.</p>\n<h2>Ready to book your truck wrap installation?</h2>\n<p>Knowing what happens at each stage of a truck wrap installation makes the whole job smoother: a clean, unwaxed truck on drop-off day, a proof that has been checked line by line, and a plan for keeping the truck warm and dry for the first day or two. If you are ready to go ahead, <a href=\"/contact/\">request a quote</a> with your truck''s details and a few photos, call us at 416-746-1381, or email <a href=\"mailto:info@VinylWrapToronto.com\">info@VinylWrapToronto.com</a>. We wrap trucks for businesses across Toronto and the GTA from our shop in Etobicoke.</p>","style":"text-align:start;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.5em;font-weight:300"}]},{"width":25,"background":"#F8F8F8","padding":"10px 10px 10px 10px","blocks":[{"type":"toc","title":"Table of Contents","box":"background-color:#F8F8F8"},{"type":"form"}]}]}]}]', datetime('now')
);
